  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Fenugreek Stalkball (Phleogena faginea) is a distinctive flowering plant endemic to New England, characterized by its spherical seed heads and delicate foliage. This rare member of the Phleogenaceae family represents an important component of northeastern woodland ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spherical Seed Head",
      "description": "Distinctive round, ball-like seed clusters that form at the stem terminals",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Finely Divided Leaves",
      "description": "Delicate, fern-like foliage with multiple small leaflets arranged alternately",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bristled Bracts",
      "description": "Sharp, hair-like projections surrounding the seed ball give it a spiky appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Fenugreek Stalkball thrives in moist, deciduous forests and shaded woodland understories throughout New England, preferring areas with rich, well-drained soils.",
